Feature: Agent Log calculated field extraction and transformation for purgo_playground.agent_log

  The Agent Log must be generated by extracting, transforming, and joining data from session_tracking_data, user_presence_tracker, and agent_profile_data, and calculating derived fields as per the mapping specification. All business rules, validation, and error handling must be strictly enforced.

  Background:
    Given the following source tables exist in Unity Catalog purgo_databricks.purgo_playground:
      | Table Name              | Columns                                                                                   |
      | session_tracking_data   | person_identifier:string, session_start_time:timestamp, session_end_time:timestamp       |
      | user_presence_tracker   | person_identifier:string, email_address:string                                           |
      | agent_profile_data      | email_address:string, internal_user_key:string, full_name:string, unixid:string, last_modified_timestamp:timestamp |
    And the target table purgo_playground.agent_log has the following columns:
      | IU_key:string, full_name:string, log_date:date, first_login:timestamp, first_logout:timestamp, total_login_time(hrs):double, city:string, state:string, country:string, zip_code:string, data_loaded_at:timestamp, unique_key:string |

  # Happy Path: All required data present, all joins succeed, all business rules met
  Scenario Outline: Successful extraction and transformation of agent log record
    Given session_tracking_data contains a record with person_identifier "<person_identifier>", session_start_time "<session_start_time>", session_end_time "<session_end_time>"
    And user_presence_tracker contains a record with person_identifier "<person_identifier>", email_address "<email_address>"
    And agent_profile_data contains one or more records with email_address "<email_address>", internal_user_key "<internal_user_key>", full_name "<full_name>", unixid "<unixid>", last_modified_timestamp "<last_modified_timestamp>"
    When the agent log is generated for log_date "<log_date>"
    Then IU_key is set to "<internal_user_key>" from the agent_profile_data record with the most recent last_modified_timestamp for email_address "<email_address>"
    And full_name is set to "<full_name>" from the agent_profile_data record with the most recent last_modified_timestamp for email_address "<email_address>"
    And log_date is set to "<log_date>" extracted as date from session_start_time
    And first_login is set to the earliest session_start_time for person_identifier "<person_identifier>" on log_date "<log_date>"
    And first_logout is set to the latest session_end_time for person_identifier "<person_identifier>" on log_date "<log_date>"
    And total_login_time(hrs) is calculated as (first_logout - first_login) in hours, rounded to 2 decimal places
    And city is set to "New York"
    And state is set to "NY"
    And country is set to "USA"
    And zip_code is set to "10001"
    And data_loaded_at is set to the current timestamp in UTC
    And unique_key is generated by concatenating all fields in order [IU_key, full_name, log_date, first_login, first_logout, total_login_time(hrs), city, state, country, zip_code, data_loaded_at] and applying SHA256 hash
    And all fields are not null except where source data is missing

    Examples:
      | person_identifier | session_start_time      | session_end_time        | email_address         | internal_user_key | full_name      | unixid   | last_modified_timestamp   | log_date    |
      | AGENT123         | 2025-01-02T08:00:00Z    | 2025-01-02T16:00:00Z    | agent1@company.com    | IUKEY001         | John Doe       | unixid1  | 2025-01-01T12:00:00Z      | 2025-01-02  |
      | AGENT456         | 2025-01-03T09:15:00Z    | 2025-01-03T17:45:00Z    | agent2@company.com    | IUKEY002         | Jane Smith     | unixid2  | 2025-01-02T09:00:00Z      | 2025-01-03  |

  # Error Path: Missing user_presence_tracker record
  Scenario: Error when user_presence_tracker record is missing for person_identifier
    Given session_tracking_data contains a record with person_identifier "AGENT789", session_start_time "2025-01-04T08:00:00Z", session_end_time "2025-01-04T16:00:00Z"
    And user_presence_tracker does not contain a record with person_identifier "AGENT789"
    When the agent log is generated for log_date "2025-01-04"
    Then IU_key is null
    And full_name is null
    And log_date is set to "2025-01-04"
    And first_login is set to "2025-01-04T08:00:00Z"
    And first_logout is set to "2025-01-04T16:00:00Z"
    And total_login_time(hrs) is calculated as 8.00
    And city is set to "New York"
    And state is set to "NY"
    And country is set to "USA"
    And zip_code is set to "10001"
    And data_loaded_at is set to the current timestamp in UTC
    And unique_key is generated by concatenating all fields (nulls included) and applying SHA256 hash

  # Error Path: Multiple agent_profile_data records for same email_address
  Scenario Outline: Select most recent agent_profile_data record for IU_key and full_name
    Given session_tracking_data contains a record with person_identifier "<person_identifier>", session_start_time "<session_start_time>", session_end_time "<session_end_time>"
    And user_presence_tracker contains a record with person_identifier "<person_identifier>", email_address "<email_address>"
    And agent_profile_data contains multiple records for email_address "<email_address>" with different last_modified_timestamp values
    When the agent log is generated for log_date "<log_date>"
    Then IU_key and full_name are set from the agent_profile_data record with the highest last_modified_timestamp

    Examples:
      | person_identifier | session_start_time      | session_end_time        | email_address         | log_date    |
      | AGENT321         | 2025-01-05T07:00:00Z    | 2025-01-05T15:00:00Z    | agent3@company.com    | 2025-01-05  |

  # Error Path: Null session_end_time
  Scenario: total_login_time(hrs) is null if first_logout is null
    Given session_tracking_data contains a record with person_identifier "AGENT654", session_start_time "2025-01-06T08:00:00Z", session_end_time null
    And user_presence_tracker contains a record with person_identifier "AGENT654", email_address "agent4@company.com"
    And agent_profile_data contains a record with email_address "agent4@company.com", internal_user_key "IUKEY004", full_name "Sam Lee", unixid "unixid4", last_modified_timestamp "2025-01-05T10:00:00Z"
    When the agent log is generated for log_date "2025-01-06"
    Then first_logout is null
    And total_login_time(hrs) is null

  # Error Path: Null session_start_time
  Scenario: first_login is null if session_start_time is null
    Given session_tracking_data contains a record with person_identifier "AGENT987", session_start_time null, session_end_time "2025-01-07T16:00:00Z"
    And user_presence_tracker contains a record with person_identifier "AGENT987", email_address "agent5@company.com"
    And agent_profile_data contains a record with email_address "agent5@company.com", internal_user_key "IUKEY005", full_name "Alex Kim", unixid "unixid5", last_modified_timestamp "2025-01-06T11:00:00Z"
    When the agent log is generated for log_date null
    Then first_login is null
    And log_date is null
    And total_login_time(hrs) is null

  # Data-driven: Multiple sessions per agent per day
  Scenario Outline: Aggregation of first_login and first_logout for multiple sessions
    Given session_tracking_data contains multiple records for person_identifier "<person_identifier>" on log_date "<log_date>" with session_start_time and session_end_time values:
      | session_start_time      | session_end_time        |
      | <session_start_time_1>  | <session_end_time_1>    |
      | <session_start_time_2>  | <session_end_time_2>    |
      | <session_start_time_3>  | <session_end_time_3>    |
    And user_presence_tracker contains a record with person_identifier "<person_identifier>", email_address "<email_address>"
    And agent_profile_data contains a record with email_address "<email_address>", internal_user_key "<internal_user_key>", full_name "<full_name>", unixid "<unixid>", last_modified_timestamp "<last_modified_timestamp>"
    When the agent log is generated for log_date "<log_date>"
    Then first_login is set to the earliest session_start_time for log_date "<log_date>"
    And first_logout is set to the latest session_end_time for log_date "<log_date>"
    And total_login_time(hrs) is calculated as (first_logout - first_login) in hours, rounded to 2 decimal places

    Examples:
      | person_identifier | log_date    | session_start_time_1   | session_end_time_1     | session_start_time_2   | session_end_time_2     | session_start_time_3   | session_end_time_3     | email_address         | internal_user_key | full_name      | unixid   | last_modified_timestamp   |
      | AGENT111         | 2025-01-08  | 2025-01-08T07:30:00Z   | 2025-01-08T12:00:00Z   | 2025-01-08T13:00:00Z   | 2025-01-08T15:00:00Z   | 2025-01-08T16:00:00Z   | 2025-01-08T18:00:00Z   | agent6@company.com    | IUKEY006         | Pat Green     | unixid6  | 2025-01-07T09:00:00Z      |

  # Validation: Data types and formats
  Scenario: Validate output data types and formats
    Given agent log record is generated
    Then IU_key, full_name, city, state, country, zip_code, unique_key are strings
    And log_date is date in format YYYY-MM-DD
    And first_login and first_logout are timestamps in format YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ
    And total_login_time(hrs) is double, rounded to 2 decimal places
    And data_loaded_at is timestamp in format YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ

  # Validation: Null handling
  Scenario Outline: Null handling for missing source values
    Given session_tracking_data contains a record with person_identifier "<person_identifier>", session_start_time "<session_start_time>", session_end_time "<session_end_time>"
    And user_presence_tracker contains a record with person_identifier "<person_identifier>", email_address "<email_address>"
    And agent_profile_data does not contain a record with email_address "<email_address>"
    When the agent log is generated for log_date "<log_date>"
    Then IU_key is null
    And full_name is null
    And unique_key is generated including nulls

    Examples:
      | person_identifier | session_start_time      | session_end_time        | email_address         | log_date    |
      | AGENT222         | 2025-01-09T08:00:00Z    | 2025-01-09T16:00:00Z    | agent7@company.com    | 2025-01-09  |

  # Validation: Hashing algorithm for unique_key
  Scenario: unique_key generation uses SHA256
    Given all fields are populated for agent log record
    When unique_key is generated
    Then the concatenated string of all fields is hashed using SHA256
    And the result is a 64-character hexadecimal string

  # Validation: Hardcoded values
  Scenario: Hardcoded values for city, state, country, zip_code
    Given agent log record is generated
    Then city is "New York"
    And state is "NY"
    And country is "USA"
    And zip_code is "10001"

  # Error Path: Invalid timestamp formats
  Scenario Outline: Error handling for invalid timestamp formats
    Given session_tracking_data contains a record with person_identifier "<person_identifier>", session_start_time "<invalid_session_start_time>", session_end_time "<invalid_session_end_time>"
    When the agent log is generated
    Then an error is logged with message "Invalid timestamp format for session_start_time or session_end_time for person_identifier <person_identifier>"
    And the agent log record is not generated

    Examples:
      | person_identifier | invalid_session_start_time | invalid_session_end_time |
      | AGENT333         | 2025-01-10 08:00           | 2025-01-10 16:00         |
      | AGENT444         | not_a_timestamp            | 2025-01-11T16:00:00Z     |

  # Error Path: Duplicate session_tracking_data records for same person_identifier and log_date
  Scenario: Deduplication of session_tracking_data records
    Given session_tracking_data contains duplicate records for person_identifier "AGENT555" and log_date "2025-01-12"
    When the agent log is generated
    Then only one agent log record is generated per person_identifier per log_date
    And first_login and first_logout are calculated from all records for that person_identifier and log_date

  # Validation: Filtering by date range
  Scenario: Only include records with session_start_time >= '2025-01-01'
    Given session_tracking_data contains records with session_start_time before and after "2025-01-01T00:00:00Z"
    When the agent log is generated
    Then only records with session_start_time >= "2025-01-01T00:00:00Z" are included

  # Error Path: Missing person_identifier in session_tracking_data
  Scenario: Error when person_identifier is null in session_tracking_data
    Given session_tracking_data contains a record with person_identifier null, session_start_time "2025-01-13T08:00:00Z", session_end_time "2025-01-13T16:00:00Z"
    When the agent log is generated
    Then an error is logged with message "Missing person_identifier in session_tracking_data"
    And the agent log record is not generated

  # Validation: All acceptance criteria
  Scenario: All acceptance criteria are met for agent log record
    Given agent log record is generated
    Then all business rules, validation rules, and error handling specified in the mapping are enforced
    And the output matches the target schema and data types
    And all required fields are populated or null as per source data
    And errors are logged for any invalid or missing data
